"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][23]={"university_physics_en:M23-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M23-L1 – Optical Cavities, Modes and Quality Factors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Bachelor: Applied Physics, Photonics and Nanotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: interference; M10:\nelectromagnetic waves. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous module: M22-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish resonance\nconditions, free spectral range and cavity quality factor and calculate\neach.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Two facing mirrors form a Fabry–Pérot cavity. Frequencies whose\nround-trip phase yields constructive interference have enhanced\ncirculating fields. The resonances depend on cavity length, refractive\nindex, loss and beam geometry, rather than the mirror coating alone. A\nlaser cavity selects longitudinal and transverse modes; the passive\noptical response can be analysed before gain is introduced.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Assume a linear cavity with constant optical path\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">nL<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand mirrors contributing negligible phase. Constructive interference\nafter a round trip requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2nL=m\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu_m=mc/(2nL)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNeighbouring longitudinal modes are separated by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℱ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mi>R<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu=\\frac{c}{2nL},\\qquad \\mathcal F\\approx\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt R}{1-R}\\quad (R\\to1).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second expression is the approximate finesse of a symmetric,\nlossless Fabry–Pérot cavity with equal intensity reflectivities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nparticularly when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproaches unity; the exact Airy linewidth also depends on the linewidth\nconvention. With resonance width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfinesse is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℱ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal F=\\Delta\\nu/\\delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy contrast\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\nu_0/\\delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrefers to the centre frequency of a single resonance. Scattering,\nabsorption, coupling and coating phase modify the measured width. In\ndispersive media, the group index rather than a constant phase index\ndetermines the mode spacing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe spacing is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.499<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu=1.499\\,\\mathrm{GHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.90<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.90<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe approximation yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℱ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>29.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal F\\approx29.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>50.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta\\nu\\approx50.3\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows a schematic cavity and calculated resonance peaks, not\nmeasured hardware data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_196_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two cavity mirrors containing a circulating ray and multiple narrow resonance peaks versus frequency.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two cavity mirrors containing a\ncirculating ray and multiple narrow resonance peaks versus\nfrequency.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Theoretical Fabry–Pérot model; relative intensities, no\nmeasurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu=2\\,\\mathrm{GHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℱ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal F=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℱ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasure different properties?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.7495<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu=c/0.40\\,\\mathrm m\\approx0.7495\\,\\mathrm{GHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta\\nu=2/40\\,\\mathrm{GHz}=50\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Finesse compares spacing to width; quality factor compares the\nresonance centre frequency to its width.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The approximation assumes a simple low-loss cavity, constant optical\nlength and high mirror reflectivity. Real coatings add dispersion,\ntemperature can change the path, and transverse modes can appear. The\nvisual is a theoretical schematic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Cavities select discrete optical modes by coherent round-trip\nfeedback. Free spectral range, finesse and quality factor describe\ndistinct aspects of that response.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Waveguides, Total Internal\nReflection and Modes<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-974-fundamentals-of-photonics-quantum-electronics-spring-2006/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Fundamentals of Photonics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 111; sessions 2–12; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L1 – Optical Cavities, Modes and Quality Factors · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-46-photonic-materials-and-devices-spring-2006/\">MIT OCW – Photonic Materials and Devices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 130–132; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L1 – Optical Cavities, Modes and Quality Factors · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M23-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M23-L2 – Waveguides, Total Internal Reflection and Modes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Bachelor: Applied Physics, Photonics and Nanotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: refraction; M10: Maxwell\nequations. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate acceptance limits for\na step-index waveguide and distinguish ray and modal descriptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>An optical fibre uses a core whose refractive index exceeds that of\nits cladding. At sufficiently oblique incidence, light undergoes total\ninternal reflection at the boundary. This ray picture is helpful for\nmultimode propagation, but the discrete patterns of a thin single-mode\nwaveguide arise from Maxwell boundary conditions. The guided field also\npenetrates evanescently into the cladding.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For two lossless isotropic media with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1&gt;n_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nSnell’s law reads\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1\\sin\\theta_1=n_2\\sin\\theta_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe critical angle measured from the interface normal, and the numerical\naperture of a step-index fibre in air, are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arcsin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_c=\\arcsin\\!\\left(\\frac{n_2}{n_1}\\right),\\qquad \\mathrm{NA}=\\sqrt{n_1^2-n_2^2}\\quad(n_0=1).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The external acceptance angle relative to the fibre axis differs from\nthe internal boundary angle measured from the normal. For launch from\nindex\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe general expression is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_0\\sin\\theta_{\\max}=\\sqrt{n_1^2-n_2^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a weakly guiding step-index fibre with core radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe dimensionless V-number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=(2\\pi a/\\lambda)\\mathrm{NA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhelps classify modes. For the ideal model, single-mode operation\napproximately requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2.405<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V&lt;2.405<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nray total reflection alone does not establish this limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.48<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1=1.48<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.46<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_2=1.46<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>1.48<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>1.46<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2425<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{NA}=\\sqrt{1.48^2-1.46^2}=0.2425<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the acceptance half-angle in air is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>14.0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14.0^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe internal critical angle from the normal is roughly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>80.6<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">80.6^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram distinguishes these angles and sketches a cladding\nevanescent field.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_316_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Fibre cross-section showing core and cladding, guided ray and a schematic boundary field.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Fibre cross-section showing core and\ncladding, guided ray and a schematic boundary field.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic optical guidance; angles and field profile not to\nscale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{NA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1=1.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.48<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_2=1.48<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{NA}=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfind the external acceptance half-angle.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is ray tracing inadequate for deriving the single-mode\nthreshold?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2.25<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2.1904<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.244<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{NA}=\\sqrt{2.25-2.1904}\\approx0.244<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arcsin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>11.54<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_{\\max}=\\arcsin(0.20)\\approx11.54^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Modes depend on field boundary conditions, wavelength and\ncross-section, not solely on reflection.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>Ray-model numerical aperture assumes ideal step-index profiles and\nlow loss. Bending, absorption, scattering, polarisation and mode\ncoupling require a full wave treatment. The diagram is schematic and not\nto scale.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Total internal reflection explains ray guidance; Maxwell boundary\nconditions explain the set of guided field modes and their\ndispersion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Thin Films, Reflection Phase and\nInterference<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-46-photonic-materials-and-devices-spring-2006/\">MIT OCW – Photonic Materials and Devices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 130–132; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L2 – Waveguides, Total Internal Reflection and Modes · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-974-fundamentals-of-photonics-quantum-electronics-spring-2006/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Fundamentals of Photonics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 111; sessions 2–12; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L2 – Waveguides, Total Internal Reflection and Modes · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M23-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M23-L3 – Thin Films, Reflection Phase and Interference<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Bachelor: Applied Physics, Photonics and Nanotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: coherent superposition; M10:\nFresnel interfaces. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive optical path differences\nin a thin film and explain the role of reflection phase.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>A transparent thin coating can reflect different colours with very\ndifferent efficiency. Partial reflections form at the front and rear\ninterfaces. The returning rays acquire a propagation path difference and\nmay also have an additional π phase from reflection at a boundary. Thus\na simple integer-wavelength rule is valid only when its phase\nassumptions are stated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>A wave traversing a homogeneous film of thickness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat internal angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacquires a propagation phase relative to the first reflection of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\phi_{\\mathrm{prop}}=(4\\pi/\\lambda_0)n_fd\\cos\\theta_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the interface-reflection phase differences cancel, imposing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\phi_{\\mathrm{prop}}=2\\pi m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(round-trip phase <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> without interface phase shift)<\/mtext><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2n_fd\\cos\\theta_f=m\\lambda_0\\qquad\\text{(round-trip phase }2\\pi m\\text{ without interface phase shift)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If exactly one reflected contribution has an additional\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphase shift, the same propagation condition is destructive rather than\nconstructive for the sum of reflected fields. At normal incidence, a\nquarter-wave antireflection film has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\lambda_0/(4n_f)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand cancellation is strong when reflected amplitudes also match. For\ntransparent materials between ambient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand substrate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan ideal single-layer design often takes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_f\\approx\\sqrt{n_0n_s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMultilayers and absorbing films are better analysed using complex\nFresnel coefficients and transfer matrices.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>A model film with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.38<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_f=1.38<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndesigned for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>550<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_0=550\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas normal-incidence thickness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>550<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.38<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>99.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=550/(4\\cdot1.38)\\,\\mathrm{nm}\\approx99.6\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figures show the reflected beams and a theoretical spectral\nreflectance dip; no coating measurement is implied.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_291_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two partial reflections from a thin film and a schematic minimum in spectral reflectance.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two partial reflections from a thin film\nand a schematic minimum in spectral reflectance.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Synthetic interference design; not a measured spectrum.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the quarter-wave thickness at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_0=600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_f=1.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2n_fd=m\\lambda_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfail to be constructive for reflection?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a path-only phase argument insufficient for strongly\nabsorbing films?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=600/(4\\cdot1.50)\\,\\mathrm{nm}=100\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A single relative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphase shift from reflection reverses the interference condition.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Absorption introduces complex refractive indices and altered field\namplitudes; transfer matrices account for both.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The simple interference rule assumes a homogeneous, isotropic, weakly\nabsorbing film and adequate coherence. Real coatings may be dispersive,\npolarisation-dependent and multilayered. A theoretical dip is not a\nguaranteed measured minimum.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Thin-film colours arise from superposing several reflected electric\nfields. Both propagation and interface-reflection phases matter.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Quantum Wells, Density of States\nand Size Quantisation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-974-fundamentals-of-photonics-quantum-electronics-spring-2006/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Fundamentals of Photonics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 111; sessions 2–12; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L3 – Thin Films, Reflection Phase and Interference · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-46-photonic-materials-and-devices-spring-2006/\">MIT OCW – Photonic Materials and Devices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 130–132; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L3 – Thin Films, Reflection Phase and Interference · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M23-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M23-L4 – Quantum Wells, Density of States and Size Quantisation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Bachelor: Applied Physics, Photonics and Nanotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: stationary Schrödinger equation;\nM14: semiconductors. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{-2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscaling of well energies and assess their size and limitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>In a semiconductor layer only a few nanometres wide, electron motion\nacross the layer can become strongly confined. Its perpendicular motion\nthen occupies discrete subband energies, while motion within the layer\nis approximately free. Quantum wells appear in lasers, detectors and\nnanoscale transport studies. An infinite potential barrier is a teaching\nmodel rather than a literal band-edge profile for all\nheterostructures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand infinitely high walls. The time-independent equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>ψ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\hbar^2\\psi&#39;&#39;/(2m^*)=E\\psi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadmits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(x)=A\\sin(kx)+B\\cos(kx)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe conditions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(0)=\\psi(L)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kL=n\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(infinite 1D well)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_n=\\frac{n^2\\pi^2\\hbar^2}{2m^*L^2},\\qquad n=1,2,3,\\ldots\\quad\\text{(infinite 1D well)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an effective band mass, not automatically the free-electron mass.\nFinite walls lower bound-state energies and allow evanescent\npenetration. With one quantised direction and two nearly free in-plane\ndirections, the simple parabolic-band 2D density of states forms steps\nat subband thresholds. Wires and quantum dots reduce the number of free\ndirections further and possess different spectral structure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Using the free-electron mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=5.0\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ideal infinite well gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1\\approx0.0150\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHalving the width to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.5\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nraises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby a factor of four to about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.0602<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.0602\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA smaller effective mass raises the quantisation energy. The plot shows\nthe well and the first three normalised standing-wave shapes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_233_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Infinite one-dimensional quantum well with three bound levels and normalised standing wavefunctions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Infinite one-dimensional quantum well\nwith three bound levels and normalised standing\nwavefunctions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Model potential and calculated wavefunctions, not an observed\nspectrum.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis tripled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_2/E_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_3/E_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin this model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot silently be replaced with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecreases by a factor of nine.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_2/E_1=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_3/E_1=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Effective mass follows the material-specific band curvature and may\ndiffer substantially from free-electron mass.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The infinite-wall, constant-mass, parabolic-band and non-interacting\nassumptions are restrictive. Real nanostructures may display excitons,\ntunnelling, roughness and nonparabolic bands. The drawn wavefunctions\nare idealised calculations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Confinement makes energies discrete through boundary conditions. The\nsquared inverse-width law is a useful first estimate for nanoscale\ndevices.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Scanning Tunnelling Microscopy\nand Distance Dependence<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-012-fundamentals-of-materials-science-fall-2005/pages/calendar/\">MIT OCW – Fundamentals of Materials Science – Calendar<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 149–155; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L4 – Quantum Wells, Density of States and Size Quantisation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-46-photonic-materials-and-devices-spring-2006/\">MIT OCW – Photonic Materials and Devices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 130–132; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L4 – Quantum Wells, Density of States and Size Quantisation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M23-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M23-L5 – Scanning Tunnelling Microscopy and Distance Dependence<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Bachelor: Applied Physics, Photonics and Nanotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: tunnelling; M14: surface states;\nM16: measurement. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain exponential\ntunnel-current sensitivity to distance and calculate an example distance\nchange.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>A scanning tunnelling microscope (STM) approaches a conductive tip\nvery close to a metallic or suitable semiconducting surface. With a bias\napplied, electrons tunnel through the narrow vacuum gap. Current is\nextremely sensitive to separation, allowing a feedback controller to\nconstruct image contrast. Atomic-resolution imaging is not a direct\nphotograph of hard spheres: the current depends on local electronic\nstates as well as the geometry.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For a one-dimensional rectangular barrier of effective height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabove the particle energy, the evanescent wavefunction behaves as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(z)\\propto e^{-\\kappa z}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nProbability density scales as amplitude squared, and under suitable\nsimplifying assumptions so does the tunnelling current, yielding<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(rectangular barrier approximation)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(z)\\propto e^{-2\\kappa z},\\qquad \\kappa\\simeq\\frac{\\sqrt{2m_e\\Phi}}{\\hbar}\\quad\\text{(rectangular barrier approximation)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At small bias and assuming a slowly varying density of states, the\nformula describes only the dominant distance dependence. Actual currents\nalso reflect bias, tip shape, tunnelling matrix elements and local\nelectronic structure. In constant-current mode, the feedback loop\nadjusts the tip height to hold current fixed. The resulting height\nrecord represents electronic topography within the model, not\nnecessarily atom-by-atom geometric positions. Too small a gap can alter\ntip or sample.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For an effective barrier height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi=4.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the free electron mass,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>10.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa\\approx10.25\\,\\mathrm{nm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIncreasing the gap by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta z=0.10\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreduces the current by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.129<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2\\kappa\\Delta z}\\approx0.129<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus an atomic-scale distance change can nearly reduce current by an\norder of magnitude. The figure depicts an ideal exponential and a\ntip–sample schematic, not real STM data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_285_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Conductive tunnelling tip above a surface and a rapidly decreasing current versus separation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Conductive tunnelling tip above a surface\nand a rapidly decreasing current versus separation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Ideal tunnelling model; not a measured atomic image.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2/I_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=10\\,\\mathrm{nm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta z=0.05\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the distance increase reducing current tenfold at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=10\\,\\mathrm{nm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an STM image not automatically a geometric height map?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.368<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2/I_1=e^{-1}\\approx0.368<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.115<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta z=\\ln(10)/(20)\\,\\mathrm{nm}\\approx0.115\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Current contrast also depends on electronic states and the condition\nof the tip.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation models a flat 1D barrier with one effective height.\nQuantitative STM spectra require a better electronic model and strict\ncontrol of mechanical noise and tip condition. The illustration is not\nan actual atomic image.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Exponential distance dependence makes STM highly sensitive, but\ninterpreting its contrast requires distinguishing geometrical and\nelectronic contributions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Solar Cells, Current–Voltage\nCurves and the Single-Diode Model<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-012-fundamentals-of-materials-science-fall-2005/pages/calendar/\">MIT OCW – Fundamentals of Materials Science – Calendar<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 149–155; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L5 – Scanning Tunnelling Microscopy and Distance Dependence · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-781j-submicrometer-and-nanometer-technology-spring-2006/pages/syllabus/\">MIT OCW – Submicrometer and Nanometer Technology – Syllabus<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 131–144; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L5 – Scanning Tunnelling Microscopy and Distance Dependence · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M23-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M23-L6 – Solar Cells, Current–Voltage Curves and the Single-Diode Model<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Bachelor: Applied Physics, Photonics and Nanotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: circuits; M14: semiconductors;\nM5: temperature. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret the implicit I–V\nrelation of an equivalent circuit and evaluate electrical output\npower.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Illumination generates electron–hole pairs in a suitable\nsemiconductor, which internal fields and selective contacts separate. A\nsolar cell is therefore neither an ideal current source nor an ideal\nvoltage source. Its I–V curve has a short-circuit current, an\nopen-circuit voltage and an intermediate maximum-power operating point.\nTemperature, irradiance, contact resistance and leakage all affect\noperation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>The standard single-diode equivalent circuit combines a\nlight-generated current source\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\mathrm L}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na diode, a shunt resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\mathrm{sh}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a series resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTaking positive current as delivered to the load gives the implicit\nrelation<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=I_{\\mathrm L}-I_0\\!\\left[\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{V+IR_s}{nV_T}\\right)-1\\right]-\\frac{V+IR_s}{R_{\\mathrm{sh}}},\\quad V_T=\\frac{k_{\\mathrm B}T}{q}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis saturation current,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na dimensionless ideality factor, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the thermal voltage of a <em>single<\/em> junction at absolute\ntemperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe internal diode voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V+IR_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseries-connected cells, the effective thermal-voltage factor includes\nthe cell count or is expressed using a modified module parameter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=N_snV_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIndividual-cell and module parameter sets must not be mixed. Since\ncurrent appears on both sides, the model generally requires a numerical\nsolution. Maximum electrical output is determined from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(V)=VI(V)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot from current or voltage alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>An idealised cell operating at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=0.50\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=3.0\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsupplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=VI=1.50\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe single-junction thermal voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>25.85<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_T=k_{\\mathrm B}T/q\\approx25.85\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram uses chosen model parameters; it is not a measured module\ncurve.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_216_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Idealised photovoltaic current–voltage and power curves with a labelled operating point.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Idealised photovoltaic current–voltage\nand power curves with a labelled operating point.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Synthetic single-diode curves, not manufacturer\nmeasurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=0.40\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=2.5\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>86.17<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_{\\mathrm B}/q\\approx86.17\\,\\mathrm{\\mu V/K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">nV_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof one cell insufficient for an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-cell\nmodule?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0.40\\cdot2.5\\,\\mathrm W=1.0\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.02585<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_T\\approx0.02585\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Series cell voltages add and module parameters must be scaled\nconsistently, for example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=N_snV_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The single-diode approximation omits numerous recombination effects\nand is not universal across technologies. Temperature and irradiance\ndependencies, shading and array topology require separate modelling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Photovoltaic I–V curves combine a light source with a nonlinear diode\nand parasitic resistances. Correct sign conventions and parameter scale\nare essential.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sensors, Noise, Filtering and\nDigitisation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-46-photonic-materials-and-devices-spring-2006/\">MIT OCW – Photonic Materials and Devices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 130–132; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L6 – Solar Cells, Current–Voltage Curves and the Single-Diode Model · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pvpmc.sandia.gov/modeling-guide/2-dc-module-iv/single-diode-equivalent-circuit-models/\">M. Boyd (NIST), C. Hansen (Sandia) – Single Diode Equivalent Circuit Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lines 143–176 · Checked: 2026-11-20; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L6 – Solar Cells, Current–Voltage Curves and the Single-Diode Model<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M23-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M23-L7 – Sensors, Noise, Filtering and Digitisation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Bachelor: Applied Physics, Photonics and Nanotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: uncertainty; M17: discrete data;\nM8: Fourier methods. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe a measurement signal\nchain and compute RC cutoff and alias frequencies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor converts a physical quantity into voltage, current,\nfrequency or counts. Before digitisation, analogue gain, impedance\nmatching, interference suppression and bandwidth limitation may be\nnecessary. An ADC does not reject high frequencies merely because its\nsample rate is low: signals outside the useful band can fold back into\nthe sampled baseband. Noise statements should specify measurement\nbandwidth and, where appropriate, spectral density rather than an\nunexplained single noise-voltage number.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For uniform sampling at frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nNyquist frequency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_{\\mathrm N}=f_s/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA passive first-order RC low-pass with negligible output loading has\ntransfer function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(j\\omega)=1/(1+j\\omega RC)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand magnitude<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_{\\mathrm N}=\\frac{f_s}{2},\\qquad |H(f)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(f/f_c)^2}},\\qquad f_c=\\frac{1}{2\\pi RC}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At cutoff, the amplitude ratio is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3.01<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3.01\\,\\mathrm{dB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn analogue antialiasing filter must attenuate unwanted input\n<em>before<\/em> sampling; a digital post-filter cannot undo aliasing\nalready created. A useful signal band to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires evaluation of transition band, stopband and worst-case\ninterfering content. Simply choosing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c&lt;f_{\\mathrm N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not enough: one RC stage rolls off only 20 dB per decade. Broadband\nnoise must be integrated over the effective noise bandwidth and\ninterpreted using the chosen spectral-density convention.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=10\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=10\\,\\mathrm{nF}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe cutoff is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1592<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c=1/(2\\pi RC)\\approx1592\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSampling at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=10\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives Nyquist frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA poorly filtered 8 kHz input tone aliases to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|8-10|=2\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nits surviving amplitude depends on analogue attenuation. The figure\ncompares frequency response and folding.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_264_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"RC low-pass preceding an ADC, its frequency response and an input tone folding to a lower alias frequency.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">RC low-pass preceding an ADC, its\nfrequency response and an input tone folding to a lower alias\nfrequency.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic frequency response and folding; not a hardware\nmeasurement.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=100\\,\\mathrm{nF}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the alias frequency of a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntone with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=10\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a digital filter <em>after<\/em> conversion insufficient as\nthe only alias protection?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RC=10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1592<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c\\approx1592\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_{\\mathrm{alias}}=|7-10|\\,\\mathrm{kHz}=3\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The ADC has already superposed the frequencies in the sampled band;\nthey cannot be unambiguously separated afterward.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The RC model assumes ideal components and negligible loading. A real\nADC front end depends on sampling architecture, input dynamics, stopband\nrejection and possibly internal oversampling. One manufacturer reference\ndesign demonstrates a particular implementation, not universal noise\nbehaviour.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Measurement chains need appropriately calibrated conditioning before\ndigitisation. Antialiasing is a system-level requirement on prefiltering\nand spectral management.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-6-007-signals-and-systems-spring-2011/pages/introduction/\">MIT OCW – Signals and Systems – Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 2–20, 26–42; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L7 – Sensors, Noise, Filtering and Digitisation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.analog.com/en/resources/reference-designs/circuits-from-the-lab/CN0535.html\">Analog Devices – CN0535 Circuit Note<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Input Signal Conditioning, lines 190–219 · Checked: 2026-11-20; Subject relevance to lesson: M23-L7 – Sensors, Noise, Filtering and Digitisation<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
